@3.4 Strings
.
=3.4 STRINGS
.
=WHAT IT COSTS
.
.  ASM: GP.INSTR GP.STRPTR GP.COMP
.    ASM -- a keyword, run by machine code in the GP runtime block. A program
.    using ANY GP keyword pays for the whole block.
.  COMPOSITE: GP.CONTAINS GP.ISEMPTY
.    COMPOSITE -- a keyword with no machine code of its own. The compiler
.    expands it into keywords that already exist, so it costs the runtime
.    nothing at all.
.
=DESCRIPTION
.
.Five keywords. GP.INSTR is the only string search GPC has; without it there
.is none.
.
.Trimming, padding and case folding are modules, not keywords: STRCASE.INC.BL
.(SEC 4.8) for case and trim in place, STRINGS.INC.BL (SEC 4.2) for padding.
.They cost 188 bytes of p-code in the programs that #INCLUDE them and nothing
.in the GP block. GP.STRPTR is the keyword they are built on.
.
.The in-place statements in STRCASE.INC.BL take a string variable, never a
.literal or an expression. The compiler rejects those. Case conversion leaves
.digits, punctuation and PETSCII graphics unchanged.
.
.There is no GP.PAD. In-place statements cannot grow a string past the
.capacity it was created with. STR.PADR / PADL / PADC (SEC 4.2) are BASIC
.assignments and do reallocate.
.
=IN THIS SECTION
.
>22|3.4.1 GP.INSTR -- position of a substring
>23|3.4.2 GP.CONTAINS -- test for a substring
>24|3.4.3 GP.ISEMPTY -- test for a zero-length string
>25|3.4.4 GP.COMP -- compare two strings, ignoring case
>26|3.4.5 GP.STRPTR -- address of a string block
.
=EXAMPLE PROGRAM
.
.  STRINGS.EXP.BL -- runnable, in the GPC-BASIC folder
.
=SEE ALSO
.
>49|4.8 STRCASE.INC.BL -- case and trim, in place
>41|4.2 STRINGS.INC.BL -- string helpers
